<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n27066" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lūcĭfŭga</span></b> ,
		ae, comm.
		lux-fugio, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>lightfleeing, light-shunning</i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: maritus,  i. e. Cupid
			(who visited Psyche only at night), <i><span class="en">App. M.
				5, p. 167, 5</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Mag. p. 284,
			38</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, <i>one who turns night
				into day</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">turba lucifugarum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Ep. 122, 15</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
